1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,283
Viral Affinity forty came on the first of December
2062, thirty-six years after a man

2
00:00:14,303 --> 00:00:19,410
opened his eyes in a leather chair with a
Viral Affinity of twelve.

3
00:00:19,809 --> 00:00:25,313
He had never bought a point of it. Every one of the
twenty-eight had been paid for in

4
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:31,031
stat points, three per level, the slow way, over
thirty-six years, while a panel offered

5
00:00:31,051 --> 00:00:34,539
him the shortcut annually and he
declined it annually.

6
00:00:35,689 --> 00:00:44,183
Status. Level three hundred and forty. Viral Affinity
forty. Strength sixty-one. Agility

7
00:00:44,203 --> 00:00:52,890
seventy-four. Vitality sixty-six. Intelligence one
hundred and fifty-one. Presence ninety.

8
00:00:52,910 --> 00:00:57,050
Will seventy-four. Requirement
met: Origin.

9
00:00:57,640 --> 00:00:59,770
Thirty-one years in a vault.

10
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:01,220
Thirty-one.

11
00:01:01,621 --> 00:01:08,847
Tell me what it does. Not what it is. What it
*does*, to me, on the fourth of December,

12
00:01:08,867 --> 00:01:14,511
at eleven o'clock at night, in a room under
a house in Pennsylvania.

13
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:17,177
(flat) I do not know.

14
00:01:17,576 --> 00:01:23,639
The narrator notes that in thirty-six years and
something over four thousand panels, this

15
00:01:23,659 --> 00:01:27,466
is the first time the System ever
says those four words.

16
00:01:27,986 --> 00:01:34,956
I know what it is. It is the strain that everything in
your blood, in that cryogenic hall,

17
00:01:34,976 --> 00:01:41,635
in Dr. Marsh's lineage and in four hundred years of
this planet's future is a copy of.

18
00:01:41,655 --> 00:01:48,236
It is not a weapon and it is not a therapy. It is
the original, and the original does

19
00:01:48,256 --> 00:01:54,293
not have a specification, because everything
that has one was derived from it.

20
00:01:54,573 --> 00:02:01,025
I know what it has done in other cases. In three
hundred and eighty-one of four hundred

21
00:02:01,045 --> 00:02:07,719
and eleven worlds the host never reached Affinity
forty. Of the thirty who did, twenty-two

22
00:02:07,739 --> 00:02:14,264
opened it. Of the twenty-two, eight died in the first
minute. Nine became something that

23
00:02:14,284 --> 00:02:20,586
I stopped being able to interface with, and I do
not have records of what happened to

24
00:02:20,606 --> 00:02:24,528
their worlds because I was no
longer present in them.

25
00:02:25,368 --> 00:02:27,739
(very cold) And the remaining two?

26
00:02:28,139 --> 00:02:30,421
(flat) Two are still running.

27
00:02:30,822 --> 00:02:31,531
Running.

28
00:02:31,931 --> 00:02:34,087
(flat) The panel is accurate.

29
00:02:34,487 --> 00:02:40,549
Two days before the vault, he put his affairs in
order, which for Albert Wesker meant

30
00:02:40,569 --> 00:02:42,337
four hours with Mr. Pell.

31
00:02:42,737 --> 00:02:48,412
The narrator mentions Pell here because he has been
in this story since Chapter One and

32
00:02:48,432 --> 00:02:54,042
has said perhaps forty words, and because what he
says in this scene is the only thing

33
00:02:54,062 --> 00:02:56,268
he ever says that is not a number.

34
00:02:56,788 --> 00:03:02,057
(flat) The instrument is complete. In the event of your
death or incapacity, the Directorate's

35
00:03:02,077 --> 00:03:07,347
chair passes to Ms. Adeyemi for ninety days, at which
point the Assembly of the Free Territory

36
00:03:07,367 --> 00:03:12,412
elects a successor by a two-thirds majority, and the
Absolute personnel are released under

37
00:03:12,432 --> 00:03:15,957
the programme with no further
instruction required from anyone.

38
00:03:16,358 --> 00:03:18,585
And if the release kills them?

39
00:03:18,985 --> 00:03:23,975
(flat) Then it kills them, Director, and the
instrument says so in clause nine, and you

40
00:03:23,995 --> 00:03:25,875
have read clause nine four times.

41
00:03:26,605 --> 00:03:31,195
Mr. Pell. You have been with me for
thirty-six years.

42
00:03:31,596 --> 00:03:34,925
(flat) Thirty-six years, two months
and eleven days.

43
00:03:35,325 --> 00:03:38,575
(very cold) You are in clause
nine yourself.

44
00:03:38,975 --> 00:03:40,225
(flat) Yes.

45
00:03:40,626 --> 00:03:42,096
Does that trouble you?

46
00:03:43,045 --> 00:03:48,464
(flat) Director, in thirty-six years you have asked
me that question twice. The first

47
00:03:48,484 --> 00:03:53,135
time was in 2027, in a lobby, about a lift
button, and you asked Mr. Gus.

48
00:03:53,415 --> 00:03:58,147
(flat) I have thought about it every day since and I
do not have an answer, because the

49
00:03:58,167 --> 00:04:02,953
part of me that would be troubled is the part that
the instrument releases, and I cannot

50
00:04:02,973 --> 00:04:04,045
get at it from here.

51
00:04:04,765 --> 00:04:09,711
(flat) I would like to find out. That is the closest
thing to a preference I have ever

52
00:04:09,731 --> 00:04:12,135
had and I am aware that it may not be one.

53
00:04:12,536 --> 00:04:18,798
Mr. Pell was released in the eleventh cohort in 2089
at the age of sixty-three. He passed

54
00:04:18,818 --> 00:04:25,080
the apprenticeship in nineteen months, which is the
slowest recorded time, and he retired

55
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:31,644
four years later to a house on the Delaware coast and
took up, according to the one interview

56
00:04:31,664 --> 00:04:37,786
he ever gave, an interest in gardening that he
described as *unsatisfactory, but mine*.

57
00:04:38,185 --> 00:04:39,665
He told four people.

58
00:04:40,065 --> 00:04:46,076
Birkin argued for six hours and produced twenty-six
reasons, all of them good, all of

59
00:04:46,096 --> 00:04:52,107
them ignored. Carrick wrote the sixth folder in
ninety minutes and hand-delivered it,

60
00:04:52,127 --> 00:04:57,216
and it said, in its entirety: *Don't. There
is nobody after you. — O.C.*

61
00:04:57,615 --> 00:05:02,225
The Red Queen ran it four hundred thousand
times and gave him a number.

62
00:05:02,745 --> 00:05:09,069
Survival, forty-one per cent. Survival with
continuity of personality, nineteen per cent.

63
00:05:09,089 --> 00:05:15,199
I would like to note that my model of you is the
best model of anything I possess, and

64
00:05:15,219 --> 00:05:20,474
that it produces a nineteen, and that I have
never asked you for anything.

65
00:05:20,754 --> 00:05:22,923
(very soft) I am asking now.

66
00:05:23,324 --> 00:05:24,194
Noted.

67
00:05:24,593 --> 00:05:26,812
That is not an answer, Director.

68
00:05:27,211 --> 00:05:33,878
(very cold) No. It is the same answer I have given
Mr. Carrick six times and it has the

69
00:05:33,898 --> 00:05:40,642
same value, which is that I have heard you, and that
you are almost certainly right, and

70
00:05:40,662 --> 00:05:43,101
that I am going to do it anyway.

71
00:05:43,502 --> 00:05:49,598
And the fourth person was Evelyn Marsh, who had taken
her own thing fifteen years earlier

72
00:05:49,618 --> 00:05:54,271
without telling anybody, and who was the
only one who did not argue.

73
00:05:54,791 --> 00:05:56,322
You'll want somebody in the room.

74
00:05:56,721 --> 00:05:57,391
No.

75
00:05:57,791 --> 00:06:02,795
You will. Not for the medicine — there isn't any
medicine, there's nothing anybody can

76
00:06:02,815 --> 00:06:07,877
do for you once that's in. For afterwards. Somebody
has to be the first person you see,

77
00:06:07,897 --> 00:06:13,368
and it should be somebody who has already been on the
other side of it, and there is precisely

78
00:06:13,388 --> 00:06:14,127
one of those.

79
00:06:14,913 --> 00:06:18,283
(very soft) And if it is not me
that wakes up?

80
00:06:18,682 --> 00:06:22,908
Then I am the only thing on this planet that
can stand in a room with it.

81
00:06:23,307 --> 00:06:28,755
The narrator notes that she was correct about that,
that both of them knew it, and that

82
00:06:28,775 --> 00:06:32,841
this is the moment the balance of the
whole account quietly tips.

83
00:06:33,901 --> 00:06:39,606
It was a glass ampoule the length of a thumb, in a
black case, on a steel table, in a

84
00:06:39,626 --> 00:06:43,581
room nine levels under a house in a
forest in Pennsylvania.

85
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:50,518
There was a chair. There was Evelyn Marsh, sitting on
the floor against the far wall with

86
00:06:50,538 --> 00:06:56,928
her arms around her knees, which is not how a
Director of a Progenitor Initiative sits,

87
00:06:56,948 --> 00:06:58,771
and which is how she sat.

88
00:06:59,170 --> 00:07:05,506
And there was Albert Wesker, thirty-six years and
eighty-one days after he had opened

89
00:07:05,526 --> 00:07:11,861
his eyes in a leather chair in an empty office in
Wilmington with ten thousand Points

90
00:07:11,881 --> 00:07:15,151
and eleven people who could not
disobey him.

91
00:07:15,550 --> 00:07:18,411
And the other one, quietly, from the
back of the skull.

92
00:07:18,930 --> 00:07:21,459
I would like to say something.

93
00:07:21,858 --> 00:07:22,528
No.

94
00:07:22,928 --> 00:07:31,267
Thirty-six years. I have asked you for four things in
thirty-six years and you have given

95
00:07:31,287 --> 00:07:35,399
me one of them, and it was a
pause in Basel.

96
00:07:36,009 --> 00:07:43,252
If you die in this room, I die in it. I would like
that noted somewhere, by somebody,

97
00:07:43,272 --> 00:07:49,746
since there is no record anywhere in this world
that I was ever here at all.

98
00:07:50,146 --> 00:07:56,329
The narrator notes it. It is the only thing the
passenger ever asks for on his own account,

99
00:07:56,349 --> 00:08:02,192
and it is not a small thing: thirty-six years riding
in a skull that was not his, in a

100
00:08:02,212 --> 00:08:08,395
world that never knew his name, having contributed —
by any honest accounting — four public

101
00:08:08,415 --> 00:08:11,736
speeches and a great deal of
advice about timing.

102
00:08:12,255 --> 00:08:13,906
(very soft) Noted.

103
00:08:15,076 --> 00:08:18,909
The narrator is not going to describe
the next nine hours.

104
00:08:19,309 --> 00:08:25,435
What is on the record is this. At eleven o'clock and
four seconds his heart stopped and

105
00:08:25,455 --> 00:08:32,006
did not restart for nineteen minutes. At eleven twenty
his body temperature reached forty-two

106
00:08:32,026 --> 00:08:38,153
point nine degrees and stayed there for four hours.
At one in the morning every monitor

107
00:08:38,173 --> 00:08:44,511
in the room failed simultaneously and permanently, in
a way that Crane's people were never

108
00:08:44,531 --> 00:08:50,658
able to explain, and from that point the only record
is Dr. Evelyn Marsh's own account,

109
00:08:50,678 --> 00:08:54,191
written eleven days later, four
pages, which ends:

110
00:08:54,711 --> 00:08:59,748
At approximately 04:10 he stopped being a shape I
recognised. I will not describe it.

111
00:08:59,768 --> 00:09:05,044
I stayed because I said I would, and because there
was no longer any question of leaving,

112
00:09:05,064 --> 00:09:10,162
and because for about forty minutes the thing in
that chair was looking at me and I do

113
00:09:10,182 --> 00:09:12,007
not believe it knew what I was.

114
00:09:12,287 --> 00:09:15,748
At 07:55 he said my name. He got it right.

115
00:09:16,697 --> 00:09:22,683
He walked out of the vault at eight o'clock in the
morning on the fifth of December 2062

116
00:09:22,703 --> 00:09:24,048
under his own power.

117
00:09:24,447 --> 00:09:30,546
He looked exactly the same. That is the part that
everybody finds hardest. No wings, no

118
00:09:30,566 --> 00:09:36,665
claws, no light under the skin. A tall man in a
black coat with slicked-back blond hair

119
00:09:36,685 --> 00:09:42,854
and a pair of black glasses, who was, by every
instrument in that building, thirty-eight

120
00:09:42,874 --> 00:09:43,557
years old.

121
00:09:44,707 --> 00:09:51,924
Host reclassified. Viral Affinity: no longer measured
on this scale. Strength, sixty-one

122
00:09:51,944 --> 00:09:59,160
to one hundred and ninety. Agility, seventy-four to
two hundred and forty. Vitality, not

123
00:09:59,180 --> 00:10:06,150
measured. Intelligence, one hundred and fifty-one,
unchanged. Note: your intelligence

124
00:10:06,170 --> 00:10:13,304
is unchanged. It was the only stat you had already
taken past what this could give you.

125
00:10:13,324 --> 00:10:16,840
Tier five. Sovereign. Orbital
tab unlocked.

126
00:10:17,431 --> 00:10:19,161
(very soft) Adolf.

127
00:10:19,990 --> 00:10:20,900
Adolf.

128
00:10:21,961 --> 00:10:23,271
There was no answer.

129
00:10:23,671 --> 00:10:29,328
The narrator would like to be precise, because there
are four wrong versions of this in

130
00:10:29,348 --> 00:10:35,071
the popular accounts. The passenger was not
destroyed, and was not silenced, and did not

131
00:10:35,091 --> 00:10:36,181
die in the chair.

132
00:10:36,581 --> 00:10:43,648
What happened is in the System's ledger, in one line,
timed at 04:10 on the fifth of December

133
00:10:43,668 --> 00:10:45,401
2062: *Merge complete.*

134
00:10:45,801 --> 00:10:51,332
For thirty-six years there had been two wills in one
skull, one of them dominant and one

135
00:10:51,352 --> 00:10:56,758
of them advisory, and they had argued about crowds
and pauses and banners. At ten past

136
00:10:56,778 --> 00:11:02,246
four in the morning the ampoule finished what the
System had started in a room that was

137
00:11:02,266 --> 00:11:08,051
not a room, and what came out of that chair at eight
o'clock was not a man with a passenger.

138
00:11:08,451 --> 00:11:15,261
It was one person. It had Albert Wesker's intellect,
Albert Wesker's discipline, Albert

139
00:11:15,281 --> 00:11:21,935
Wesker's thirty-six-year record of never having
killed anybody in this world — and it

140
00:11:21,955 --> 00:11:29,472
had, folded into it now and indistinguishable, an
absolute and entirely untroubled understanding

141
00:11:29,492 --> 00:11:30,258
of crowds.

142
00:11:30,658 --> 00:11:36,733
He stood on the terrace above the fog, in the same
place he had stood in May of 2027 when

143
00:11:36,753 --> 00:11:41,048
a house full of ghosts had been handed
to him out of a lottery.

144
00:11:41,567 --> 00:11:42,597
Are you still in there?

145
00:11:42,998 --> 00:11:43,968
Yes.

146
00:11:44,368 --> 00:11:45,009
Prove it.

147
00:11:45,408 --> 00:11:51,177
And Albert Wesker turned around and looked at the
only other creature on earth that could

148
00:11:51,197 --> 00:11:53,128
have asked him that, and said:

149
00:11:53,649 --> 00:12:01,305
In 2029 you asked me for this and I said no, and you
asked why, and I said it would kill

150
00:12:01,325 --> 00:12:08,719
you. In 2038 you asked again and I said no and gave
you a condition. In 2047 you took

151
00:12:08,739 --> 00:12:16,308
it anyway and I stood at a window for twenty-four
hours and said one word, and the word

152
00:12:16,328 --> 00:12:21,019
was *no*, and there was nobody in
the room to hear it.

153
00:12:21,739 --> 00:12:28,189
(very soft) I am still in here, Dr. Marsh. I
would like you to keep checking.

154
00:12:28,589 --> 00:12:35,060
She checked for twenty-nine years. She never once
found anything, and she said so, every

155
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:38,159
year, in a one-line note that Wesker kept.

156
00:12:38,558 --> 00:12:43,423
The narrator will point out what the panel said,
though, because nobody in that house

157
00:12:43,443 --> 00:12:45,548
read it carefully enough at the time.

158
00:12:45,948 --> 00:12:52,871
*Intelligence: unchanged.* Everything else about Albert
Wesker was rebuilt from the original

159
00:12:52,891 --> 00:12:59,512
in nine hours. The one thing the Progenitor could not
improve was the thing he had spent

160
00:12:59,532 --> 00:13:03,058
thirty-six years buying three
points at a time.

161
00:13:03,458 --> 00:13:08,780
He had made himself, by hand, into the only part of
himself that could not be given to

162
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:09,028
him.

163
00:13:09,428 --> 00:13:10,688
End of Part Four.
